# The harness's task-killed notice, as `claude -p --output-format stream-json
# --verbose` writes it (mission 8 U2, DESIGN §5, §24; backlog item 1).
#
# OBSERVED. Every non-comment line below is copied from a real hands stream log
# (~/.hands/jobs/<job>.stream.jsonl, the runner's verbatim capture of stdout):
#
#   A. job 0mtygi953-ym63 (builder), claude 2.1.269 (its init event), lines
#      1406, 1408, 1413, 1414: a Bash command (task bar46gi30) owned by a
#      sub-agent, killed at the same millisecond as that sub-agent.
#   B. job 0mtyvy78g-yq73 (builder), claude 2.1.270, lines 608, 609, 621, 635,
#      636: a Bash command (task bs3zkkxlo) the harness moved to the background
#      after the Bash tool's timeout, then stopped with the TaskStop tool.
#
# The `system` lines are byte for byte. The `assistant` lines are reduced: the
# `tool_use` content item is the recorded one (re-serialised by json.dumps),
# while the rest of the recorded message (model, usage, ids) is left out; the
# runner reads nothing else from an assistant event.
#
# The notice is two events per killed task: `system`/`task_updated` whose
# `patch.status` is "killed", then `system`/`task_notification` whose `status`
# is "stopped". The binary (2.1.270, `strings`) maps one to the other when a
# local shell task settles: `Hi(e,r==="killed"?"stopped":r,{toolUseId:m,...})`,
# and the SDK schema for task_notification's status is
# `V(["completed","failed","stopped"])` - there is no "killed" on that event.
# The command line is in neither: it is the `input.command` of the `Bash`
# tool_use whose id is the task's `tool_use_id` (on task_started and on
# task_notification).
#
# NOT OBSERVED: a kill the harness makes on its own schedule with nothing else
# involved. A and B were killed with a parent agent and by TaskStop; the stream
# carries no field saying who killed a task, so the shape is the same for all
# three and hands cannot tell them apart.
